  "query_preview": "..\n\nMostly clear skies this morning with dissipating debris clouds\nfrom yesterdays convection. Today will be a less active day than\nyesterday but there is a chance for showers and thunderstorms\nfor areas east and south of Tucson. Satellite derived PWATs \nshow moisture is concentrated to areas east of the \nCochise/Graham county line. Current PWAT values are around 0.6 \nto 0.8 inches in Tucson increasing eastward with values around \n0.8 to 1.0 inches closer to the AZ/NM border. Surface dewpoints\nTucson eastward are around 45-50 degrees with values decreasing\nwestward with values in the 20s by Sells.\n\nThe bulk of activity today will be in Cochise, Graham, Greenlee,\nand Santa Cruz counties with the fringes of activity in eastern\nPima and Pinal counties. Initiation for convection will begin \nalong the mountains around noon to 1 PM and then move into the \nvalleys. The bulk of storm activity will be between 1 to 7 PM \nMST.\n\nThe main concern associated with convection today is gusty\nwinds and blowing dust. Low level moisture is again dryer than \nthe mid levels producing inverted V soundings with forecast \nDCAPE between 1000-1200 J/kg. HREF 4 hr max wind speed has \n30-70% chance for greater than 30 kts (40 MPH) Tucson eastward. \nThe potential for strong winds, some thunderstorms may produce \nisolated wind gusts between 50-60 mph, will lead to a concern \nfor blowing dust especially in dust prone areas. \n\nLike yesterday, the concern for flash flooding is low. Steering\nwinds today are fairly low with 0-6 km wind speeds around 5-8 \nkts to the southwest suggesting storms will be moving very \nslowly. Some of the thunderstorms may produce brief, locally \nmoderate to heavy rainfall with amounts between 0.25 to 0.5 \ninches.\n\nMoisture will quickly move out of the area after today returning\nto dry conditions and slightly above normal high temperatures.",
